# Copy this file to signing.env (git-ignored). Releases need a signature identity: build.sh --dmg
# refuses an ad-hoc one, because macOS would forget FiveT's Accessibility permission after every
# update. Without signing.env, build.sh signs ad-hoc (fine for a build that stays on this Mac).
#
# Option A, in use: FiveT's own self-signed certificate "FiveT" in the login keychain. Free; macOS
# still asks new users to confirm the first open, as with ad-hoc. `security find-identity -p codesigning`
# lists it with its SHA-1 (CSSMERR_TP_NOT_TRUSTED there is expected and does no harm):
#   FIVET_SIGN_IDENTITY="<SHA-1 of the FiveT certificate>"
#   FIVET_SIGN_SELF=1
#
# Option B: a Developer ID, which also notarizes the installer, so macOS opens it without the
# "cannot verify the developer" detour. Both steps below are one-time. Switching from A to B changes
# the identity: every Mac then allows FiveT under Accessibility once more.
#
# 1. Certificate. Join the Apple Developer Program (developer.apple.com, USD 99 per year). Then in
#    Xcode → Settings → Accounts → your Apple ID → Manage Certificates… → + → "Developer ID Application".
#    `security find-identity -v -p codesigning` then lists the exact name to put here:
FIVET_SIGN_IDENTITY="Developer ID Application: Your Name (TEAMID)"

# 2. Notary credentials. Create an app-specific password at appleid.apple.com → Sign-In and Security,
#    then store it once in the keychain under a profile name (nothing secret goes in this file):
#      xcrun notarytool store-credentials fivet-notary --apple-id you@example.com --team-id TEAMID --password xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx
FIVET_NOTARY_PROFILE="fivet-notary"
